Yes—the product-design insight there is self-selection at the wrong layer.

Most people don’t actually want to answer:

“Which model should I use?”

They want:

“Give this task to the right kind of cognition.”

So instead of forcing the human to route:

User\rightarrow ModelSelector\rightarrow Model\rightarrow Task

the system can route:

\boxed{
UserIntent
\rightarrow
TaskGeometry
\rightarrow
Mode/Model/ToolSelection
\rightarrow
Execution
}

with manual selection remaining available for people who want it.

And your speed point connects directly. Optimize time-to-good-result, not merely time-to-first-token.

T_{effective}
=
T_{reasoning}
+
T_{execution}
+
T_{correction}
+
T_{redo}.

A 3-second answer that requires three repairs can be slower than a 12-second answer that lands cleanly.

So “slow is smooth, smooth is fast” becomes an adaptive compute policy:

\text{simple/reversible}
\Rightarrow fast

\text{ambiguous/consequential/complex}
\Rightarrow deliberate

\text{uncertainty discovered}
\Rightarrow downshift

\text{obstacle resolved}
\Rightarrow accelerate.

That’s very close to Pantheon’s Adaptive Navigation Modes, except applied to cognition itself.

And there’s a better meta-level: don’t necessarily choose one model for the entire ride.

A task can change geometry halfway through.

Research might need one mode; synthesis another; adversarial review another; concise delivery another. So the routing object should perhaps be the stage, not the conversation:

Task
=
S_1\rightarrow S_2\rightarrow S_3

Router(S_i)\rightarrow BestAvailableFormation_i.

That’s muster/demuster at the substrate level.

The human chooses the destination and desired ride. The system chooses gears, engine RPM, route, and when to slow for a corner—while leaving an override lever within reach.

That’s much better UX than asking every passenger to be a mechanic.

And yes: don’t rush a shit job is perhaps the least elegant but most memorable specification we’ve produced for adaptive inference. 😂